NEWS file for libxpath3

v0.2.0: Nov 11 2026

### Function library, core

libxpath3 0.2 evaluates function calls, with the core of the F&O 3.1
function library and functions the application registers.

- Function calls apply the function conversion rules. Calls with
  constant arguments are folded when the function allows it.
- The basic functions: `true`, `false`, `not`, `boolean`, `string`,
  `data`, `number`, `position`, `last`, `error`, `default-collation`,
  the cardinality checks and the sequence functions `empty`, `exists`,
  `count`, `head`, `tail`, `reverse`, `subsequence`, `remove`,
  `insert-before`, `distinct-values`, `index-of` and `deep-equal`.
- Numeric functions: `abs`, `ceiling`, `floor`, `round`,
  `round-half-to-even`, `sum`, `avg`, `min`, `max`, and the `math:`
  functions.
- String functions without regular expressions, including
  `string-join`, `substring`, `contains`, `translate`, `compare`,
  `normalize-space`, `contains-token` and `tokenize#1`, and the URI
  functions `encode-for-uri`, `iri-to-uri` and `escape-html-uri`.
  They work under the Unicode codepoint collation and the HTML ASCII
  case-insensitive collation; UCA collation URIs fall back to
  codepoint order.
- Functions on nodes: `name`, `local-name`, `namespace-uri`,
  `node-name`, `lang`, `root`, `path`, `has-children`, `innermost`,
  `outermost`, `base-uri`, `document-uri`, `generate-id`,
  `in-scope-prefixes` and `namespace-uri-for-prefix`.
- `xp3RegisterFunction()` adds extension functions with an F&O-style
  signature, such as `u:f($a as xs:string?) as xs:decimal`. Arguments
  and results go through the same conversion rules as built-in
  functions. `xp3FunctionError()` raises an error from inside one, and
  `xp3SetDynamicContextUserData()` passes data to it per evaluation.
- `xp3SetMaxSteps()` limits the work of one evaluation, for
  applications that evaluate untrusted expressions. String work
  counts by the bytes read and made. There is no limit by default.
- `xp3SequenceRef()` keeps a sequence, and `xp3NewAtomicSequence()`
  builds values of any built-in atomic type from their lexical forms.

### Conformance

Against the W3C QT3 test suite at commit 201a6e4, compiling passes all
XPath 2.0 tests and all but one known failure at 3.0 and 3.1.
Evaluating, XPath 3.1 has 11,171 tests passed, 59 failed and 10,801
not judged yet. 56 of the failures need UCA collations with real
tailorings.

### Not yet supported

- Regular expressions and the functions that use them.
- Date, time and duration values.
- Upper- and lower-case mapping, Unicode normalization, the QName
  functions, `resolve-uri`, `format-number` and `doc`.
- Maps, arrays, function items and the higher-order functions.

The namespace axis is not supported by design (XPST0010).

v0.1.0: Oct 29 2026

### First release

libxpath3 evaluates XPath 2.0, 3.0 and 3.1 expressions directly on
libxml2's tree. This first release covers the language without its
function library.

- The parser handles the complete XPath 3.1 grammar, with a language
  level for 2.0 and 3.0.
- Static analysis resolves namespaces, variables and the signatures of
  the F&O 3.1 functions, and raises the static errors of the
  specification.
- The evaluator handles paths and predicates, `for`, `let`, `some`,
  `every` and `if`, the logical, comparison and arithmetic operators,
  ranges, `union`, `intersect` and `except`, `cast`, `castable`,
  `instance of` and `treat`.
- `xs:decimal` and `xs:integer` have arbitrary precision. Decimal
  division rounds as Saxon does.
- Constant folding and path rewrites keep results in document order
  without sorting where possible.
- Limits on nesting depth, sequence length and the work of constant
  folding make it safe to compile untrusted expressions. A compiled
  expression can be evaluated by several threads at once, each with
  its own dynamic context.
- An API for language bindings reads results as numbers, booleans and
  strings with their atomic types, and builds sequences of them to
  bind as variables.

### Conformance

Against the W3C QT3 test suite at commit 201a6e4, compiling passes all
XPath 2.0 tests and all but one known failure at 3.0 and 3.1.
Evaluating, XPath 3.1 has 5,346 tests passed, 3 failed and 16,682 not
judged yet, mostly because they call functions.

### Not yet supported

- Function calls are compiled but not evaluated yet.
- Date, time and duration values, regular expressions, maps, arrays
  and function items.
- A limit on the run time of an evaluation.

The namespace axis is not supported by design (XPST0010).
